<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <!--
      docs/plan/03 § Providers — the first of the five projects: "grain interfaces, resource models,
      the JSON Schemas". This one is the schema and the CloudNativePG projection.

      ⚠ NO GRAIN INTERFACES, AND FOR THE SAME REASON CyberCloud.Providers.Sample.Contracts HAS NONE:
      a server's desired state is held by CyberCloud.ResourceManager's ResourceGrain, and a grain of
      our own would be a second copy of state the manager already owns. That is worth restating on
      the FIRST NON-TRIVIAL PROVIDER, because the sample's absence of a grain could be read as a
      consequence of the sample being trivial. It is not: a managed PostgreSQL server with backup,
      pooling, extensions and a sizing preset still needs no grain, which is evidence about the
      manager rather than about the resource. docs/plan/25 § R1 asks for exactly that evidence.

      ⚠ IT REFERENCES ONLY CyberCloud.ResourceManager.Contracts AND CyberCloud.Kubernetes.Contracts —
      the whole reference set docs/plan/03 § The .Contracts split allows a provider. It references NO
      OTHER Providers.* assembly, which is rule 2 of docs/plan/03 § Assembly graph rules; that rule
      had three candidates in one family before this project existed and now has candidates in two,
      so a violation of it is constructible for the first time.

      ⚠ RULE 2 HAD A BLIND SPOT AND THIS PROJECT IS WHERE IT BECAME FINDABLE. CLOSED 2026-08-12;
      the experiment is kept because it is the evidence, and because the same reasoning constrains
      every future rule phrased over metadata. The gate read Assembly.ReferencedAssemblies — the
      BINDING references in the compiled IL — so it fired on "binds types from". A cross-provider
      dependency that touches only `const` members emits NO reference at all, because the C#
      compiler inlines a const into the consuming assembly. Verified by adding a real
      ProjectReference to CyberCloud.Providers.Sample.Contracts here and reading one member: with
      `SampleWidgets.TypePath` (a const string) rule 2 reported 6 candidates and ZERO violations and
      the build was green; changing it to `SampleWidgets.Type` (a static property) made the same edit
      fail with "binds types from CyberCloud.Providers.Sample.Contracts".

      Rules 2, 4, 5 and 7 now read the declared ProjectReference set as well as the binding
      references — Build.Architecture.cs § InTreeEdges — so for an in-tree edge the reference itself
      is the violation and the const variant of the experiment above fails too. Rule 3 deliberately
      does NOT get the same treatment: docs/plan/02 § ADR-004 puts KubernetesClient in the restore
      closure legitimately, so only the binding half of that rule is satisfiable.

      ⚠ THE GENERAL LESSON OUTLIVES THE FIX. A rule that reads metadata sees only what survives to
      metadata, and `const` is not the only thing that does not: `nameof`, a literal attribute
      argument and a `typeof` that is only compared all vanish too. What closed this hole was not
      finding every such construct, it was reading a second source that records intent rather than
      use. Any new rule about our own assemblies should do the same.
    -->

    <ItemGroup>
        <ProjectReference Include="../../../CyberCloud.ResourceManager.Contracts/CyberCloud.ResourceManager.Contracts.csproj"/>
        <ProjectReference Include="../../../CyberCloud.Kubernetes.Contracts/CyberCloud.Kubernetes.Contracts.csproj"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup Label="CyberCloud.Analyzers — see CyberCloud.Core.csproj for why this is not a reference">
        <ProjectReference Include="../../../CyberCloud.Analyzers/CyberCloud.Analyzers.csproj"
                          OutputItemType="Analyzer"
                          ReferenceOutputAssembly="false"/>
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
